{"name":"Paw Paw","hex":"#fbd49c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Paw Paw","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A village in Lee County, Illinois, United States.","A ghost town in Miami County, Indiana, United States."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of paw paw (“pawpaw, grandfather”)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Paw_Paw"}],"entries":[{"title":"Asimina","blocks":[{"text":"Asimina is a genus of small trees or shrubs described as a genus in 1763. Asimina is the only temperate genus in the tropical and subtropical flowering plant family Annonaceae. Asimina have large, simple leaves and large fruit. It is native to eastern North America and collectively referred to as pawpaw. Asimina includes the widespread common pawpaw A. triloba, which bears the largest edible fruit indigenous to the United States. Pawpaws are native to 26 states of the U.S. and to Ontario in Canada. The common pawpaw is a patch-forming (clonal) understory tree found in well-drained, deep, fertile bottomland and hilly upland habitat. Pawpaws are in the same plant family (Annonaceae) as the custard apple, cherimoya, sweetsop, soursop, and ylang-ylang; the genus is the only member of that family not confined to the tropics. Fossils date to the Cretaceous."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Asimina_triloba3.jpg/330px-Asimina_triloba3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asimina","wikidata":"Q288443"},{"title":"Asimina triloba","blocks":[{"text":"Asimina triloba, the American papaw, pawpaw, paw paw, or paw-paw, among many regional names, is a species of small deciduous tree. It has large leaves and produces a large, yellowish-green to brown fruit."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Asimina_triloba3.jpg/330px-Asimina_triloba3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asimina_triloba","wikidata":"Q948827"},{"title":"Paw","blocks":[{"text":"A paw is the soft foot-like part of a mammal, generally a quadruped, that has claws. To paw is for an animal to use their paws, usually gently or for digging, or for a human to use their hands in the manner of an animal's paw."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Paw_and_pads.jpg/330px-Paw_and_pads.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paw","wikidata":"Q219588"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Michel_Adanson_%281727-1806%29_crop.png/500px-Michel_Adanson_%281727-1806%29_crop.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Michel_Adanson_%281727-1806%29_crop.png/500px-Michel_Adanson_%281727-1806%29_crop.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Michel Adanson (1727–1806), who named the genus Asimina","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMichel_Adanson_(1727-1806)_crop.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Asimina_382343561.jpg/500px-Asimina_382343561.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Asimina_382343561.jpg/1280px-Asimina_382343561.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Asimina_382343561.jpg/500px-Asimina_382343561.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Flower of a natural hybrid Asimina from Gainesville, FL","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAsimina_382343561.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Asimina_reticulata_flower.jpg/3